# 🧠 What is OOP?

**OOP = Object-Oriented Programming**

Java is an object-oriented programming language.

OOP organizes programs using **classes and objects**.

### Main OOP concepts:

1. Class
2. Object
3. Encapsulation
4. Inheritance
5. Polymorphism
6. Abstraction

Today we started with:

> **Class → Object → Instance Variables → Methods**

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 1️⃣ Create a Class and Object

### 🎯 Problem

Create a class with `name` and `age`, create an object, and assign values.

### Code

```java
class CreateClassAndObject {

    String name;
    int age;

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        CreateClassAndObject s = new CreateClassAndObject();

        s.name = "Rahul";
        s.age = 21;

        System.out.println("Name = " + s.name);
        System.out.println("Age = " + s.age);
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Name = Rahul
Age = 21
```

### 🧠 Key Concept

```java
CreateClassAndObject s = new CreateClassAndObject();
```

This creates an object.

Breakdown:

```text
CreateClassAndObject → class type
s                    → object reference
new                   → creates object
CreateClassAndObject() → constructor call
```

### 💡 Memory Trick

> **Class = Blueprint** 🏗️  
> **Object = Real thing created from blueprint**

### Complexity

- Time: `O(1)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 2️⃣ Create Two Objects from the Same Class

### 🎯 Problem

Create two Student objects with different values.

### Code

```java
class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s1 = new Student();
        Student s2 = new Student();

        s1.name = "Rahul";
        s1.age = 21;

        s2.name = "Arjun";
        s2.age = 22;

        System.out.println("Name = " + s1.name);
        System.out.println("Age = " + s1.age);

        System.out.println("Name = " + s2.name);
        System.out.println("Age = " + s2.age);
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Name = Rahul
Age = 21
Name = Arjun
Age = 22
```

### 🧠 Important Concept

One class can create **many objects**.

```text
Student class
     ↓
 ┌─────────┐
 │   s1    │ → Rahul, 21
 └─────────┘

 ┌─────────┐
 │   s2    │ → Arjun, 22
 └─────────┘
```

Each object has its **own data**.

### 💡 Interview Tip

> A class is a blueprint; an object is an instance of that class.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 3️⃣ Create and Call a Method

### 🎯 Problem

Create a method `display()` that prints student information.

### Code

```java
class VoidStudent {

    String name;
    int age;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(age);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        VoidStudent s = new VoidStudent();

        s.name = "Rahul";
        s.age = 21;

        s.display();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Rahul
21
```

### 🧠 Key Concept

A method represents an **action/behavior** of an object.

```text
Student
│
├── name → data
├── age  → data
│
└── display() → behavior
```

### Memory Trick

> **Variables = What an object HAS**  
> **Methods = What an object DOES**

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 4️⃣ Calculate Student's Next Age

### 🎯 Problem

Use a method to calculate the student's age next year.

### Code

```java
class NextAge {

    String name;
    int age;

    void nextAge() {
        System.out.println(
            name + "'s age next year = " + (age + 1)
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        NextAge s = new NextAge();

        s.name = "Rahul";
        s.age = 21;

        s.nextAge();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Rahul's age next year = 22
```

### 🧠 Pattern

```text
Object data
    ↓
age = 21
    ↓
Method
    ↓
age + 1
    ↓
22
```

### Important

The calculation belongs inside the method because the method is responsible for the action.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 5️⃣ Check Passing Grade

### 🎯 Problem

Check whether a student passes.

Condition:

```text
marks >= 40 → Pass
marks < 40  → Fail
```

### Code

```java
class PassingGrade {

    String name;
    int marks;

    void checkResult() {

        if(marks >= 40) {
            System.out.println(name + ": Pass");
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(name + ": Fail");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        PassingGrade s = new PassingGrade();

        s.name = "Rahul";
        s.marks = 65;

        s.checkResult();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Rahul: Pass
```

### 🧠 Key Concept

A method can contain normal programming logic such as:

- `if`
- `else`
- loops
- calculations

OOP does not replace these concepts—it organizes them inside classes and objects.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 6️⃣ Find Largest of Two Numbers

### 🎯 Problem

Find the larger of two object variables.

### Code

```java
class LargestOfTwo {

    int a;
    int b;

    void findLargest() {

        if(a > b) {
            System.out.println("Largest = " + a);
        }
        else {
            System.out.println("Largest = " + b);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        LargestOfTwo s = new LargestOfTwo();

        s.a = 25;
        s.b = 40;

        s.findLargest();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Largest = 40
```

### 🧠 Pattern

```text
a > b → a is largest
otherwise → b is largest
```

### Complexity

- Time: `O(1)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 7️⃣ Check Even or Odd Using a Method

### 🎯 Problem

Check whether an object's number is even or odd.

### Code

```java
class CheckEvenOrOdd {

    int number;

    void checkEvenOdd() {

        if(number % 2 == 0) {
            System.out.println("Even");
        }
        else {
            System.out.println("Odd");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        CheckEvenOrOdd s = new CheckEvenOrOdd();

        s.number = 24;

        s.checkEvenOdd();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Even
```

### 🧠 Remember

```java
number % 2 == 0
```

→ Even

```java
number % 2 != 0
```

→ Odd

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 8️⃣ Calculate Square Using a Method

### 🎯 Problem

Calculate the square of an object's number.

### Code

```java
class SquareOfNum {

    int number;

    void calculateSquare() {

        int square = number * number;

        System.out.println("Square = " + square);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        SquareOfNum s = new SquareOfNum();

        s.number = 7;

        s.calculateSquare();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Square = 49
```

### 🧠 Important Concept: `void`

```java
void calculateSquare()
```

`void` means:

> The method does not return a value.

But variables inside the method **still need data types**:

```java
int square = number * number;
```

### Important distinction

```text
void
 ↓
Method return type

int
 ↓
Variable data type
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 9️⃣ Calculate Cube Using a Method

### 🎯 Problem

Calculate the cube of a number.

### Code

```java
class CubeOfNum {

    int number;

    void calculateCube() {

        int cube = number * number * number;

        System.out.println("Cube = " + cube);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        CubeOfNum s = new CubeOfNum();

        s.number = 4;

        s.calculateCube();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Cube = 64
```

### 🧠 Formula

```text
Square = number × number

Cube = number × number × number
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🔟 Calculate Sum of Two Numbers Using a Method

### 🎯 Problem

Calculate the sum of two object variables.

### Code

```java
class SumOfTwoNumbers {

    int a;
    int b;

    void calculateSum() {

        int sum = a + b;

        System.out.println("Sum = " + sum);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        SumOfTwoNumbers s = new SumOfTwoNumbers();

        s.a = 25;
        s.b = 15;

        s.calculateSum();
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Sum = 40
```

### 🧠 Pattern

```text
Object
 ↓
a = 25
b = 15
 ↓
calculateSum()
 ↓
25 + 15
 ↓
40
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🧠 DAY 7 — MOST IMPORTANT CONCEPTS

## 1. Class

A class is a **blueprint/template**.

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 2. Object

An object is an **instance of a class**.

```java
Student s = new Student();
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 3. Instance Variables

Variables declared inside a class but outside methods are commonly called **instance variables**.

```java
class Student {

    String name;
    int age;
}
```

Each object gets its own values.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 4. Method

A method defines an action/behavior.

```java
void display() {
    System.out.println(name);
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🔥 The Complete OOP Pattern

This is the most important pattern from Day 7:

```text
              CLASS
                ↓
        ┌───────────────┐
        │ Variables     │
        │ Methods       │
        └───────────────┘
                ↓
          CREATE OBJECT
                ↓
        Assign object data
                ↓
          CALL METHOD
                ↓
       Method performs task
```

Example:

```java
Student s = new Student();

s.name = "Rahul";
s.age = 21;

s.display();
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🧠 COMMON MISTAKES FROM TODAY

## ❌ Mistake 1 — Putting instance variables inside a method

Wrong:

```java
void display() {
    String name;
    int age;
}
```

These are **local variables**.

Correct:

```java
class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## ❌ Mistake 2 — Calling the wrong method name

Java is **case-sensitive**.

```java
void nextAge()
```

must be called as:

```java
s.nextAge();
```

Not:

```java
s.NextAge();
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## ❌ Mistake 3 — Forgetting to assign object values

Wrong:

```java
Student s = new Student();

s.display();
```

If values were never assigned, default values may be used.

Correct:

```java
Student s = new Student();

s.name = "Rahul";
s.age = 21;

s.display();
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## ❌ Mistake 4 — Confusing `void` with variable data types

Wrong idea:

> "Because the method is void, variables don't need data types."

Correct:

```java
void calculateSquare() {

    int square = number * number;
}
```

`void` → method return type.

`int` → variable data type.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

